var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Cards","page":"Cards","text":"A FITS header is a list of 80-character cards, or keyword records. A Card holds the parts of one card, the keyword, the value, the units, and the comment, and the layout that is needed to write the card as it was read. This page describes the creation, the parsing, and the modification of cards. The rules are those of the FITS Standard 4.0, Sect. 4, and of the ESO HIERARCH convention.\n\nThe examples use the following definitions, where show_ prints the 80-character image of a card without the trailing spaces.","title":"Cards"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Creating-a-card","page":"Cards","text":"A card is created from a keyword, a value, and a comment, which are all optional.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\");\n\njulia> show_(card)\nEXPTIME =               1200.0 / exposure time\n\njulia> card.key, card.value, card.units, card.comment\n(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, nothing, \"exposure time\")\n\njulia> typeof(card)\nCard{Value{Float64}}\n\nThe type of the card is the kind of the card, Value, Hierarch, Comment, History, Continue, End, or Invalid, and for a value the type of the value.\n\nValue Card\nmissing, or none an undefined value\ntrue, false a logical\nInteger an integer\nAbstractFloat a real number\nComplex of integers or of floats a complex number\nAbstractString a string\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"NAXIS\", 2))\nNAXIS   =                    2\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"OBJECT\", \"NGC 1275\", \"target\"))\nOBJECT  = 'NGC 1275'           / target\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"FLAG\", true))\nFLAG    =                    T\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"Z\", 1.5 + 2.5im))\nZ       = (1.5, 2.5)\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"EMPTY\"))\nEMPTY   =\n\nOther values, e.g., a Rational or Complex{Bool}, are an ArgumentError, and a number that is not finite is a DomainError, since the FITS Standard has no such values. The text of a string and of a comment is the printable ASCII characters, 32 to 126.\n\nA keyword is converted to upper case. A keyword with the characters A-Z, 0-9, -, and _ and at most 8 characters is a regular keyword. A longer keyword is a HIERARCH keyword.","title":"Creating a card"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Keyword-arguments","page":"Cards","text":"The keyword arguments of Card format the card. The word keyword also means the name of a card, so a keyword argument is called a keyword here only if it is clear. The arguments are the layout of the card, and the layout of a card that is parsed is read from the image.\n\nKeyword Default Function\nfixed true Use the fixed format, where a number ends at byte 30 and a string begins at byte 11. The free format has the value anywhere after byte 10. The mandatory keywords are always in fixed format. See Fixed and free format.\nslash 32 The index, in bytes, of the comment separator /. A value that passes the index moves the separator and the comment to the right, and a separator at the end of the card has no comment, unless truncate = false, where the comment is continued at index 32. A number in fixed format ends at byte 30, so its separator is at byte 32 or later.\nlpad 1 The least number of spaces between the value and the comment separator.\nrpad 1 The number of spaces between the comment separator and the units or the comment.\nupad 1 The number of spaces between the units and the comment.\ntruncate true Truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card. If false, the comment of a string is continued on CONTINUE cards, and a comment of another value, and units, that do not fit are an ArgumentError. See Long strings.\nsplitat ' ' Split a long string and its comment after the last character splitat that fits, or at any character if nothing. See Long strings.\nappend false Append an ampersand to the string, which marks that a CONTINUE card continues the string. A string that is too long for a card has the ampersand without the keyword.\nwarn true Warn when a keyword with more than 8 characters creates a HIERARCH card.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", 1, \"comment\"))\nKEY     =                    1 / comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", 1, \"comment\"; slash = 20, lpad = 0, rpad = 3))\nKEY     =                    1/   comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", 1, \"comment\"; fixed = false))\nKEY     = 1                    / comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"KEY\", \"abc\", \"comment\"; append = true))\nKEY     = 'abc&'               / comment\n\nsetvalue! has the same keyword arguments, except warn, and the default of a layout keyword argument is the layout of the card, see Changing the value. The function parse has the keywords strict, which allows a lower case exponent letter if false, and units, which reads the units from the comment if true, see Parsing an image.","title":"Keyword arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Units","page":"Cards","text":"A Quantity is not a type of value. The units of a quantity are not part of the value, so a Quantity that is given in place of a number is split into its number and its units, and the type of the card is the type of the number, e.g., the type of a card with 1200.0u\"s\" is Card{Value{Float64}}. The units are read from the comment when a card is parsed, where the units are in square brackets at the beginning of the comment (Sect. 4.3.2), and they are read in the same way from the comment of a number that is given to Card: the unit string is removed from the comment and is the units of the card, as if the number were a Quantity.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0u\"s\", \"exposure time\");\n\njulia> show_(card)\nEXPTIME =               1200.0 / [s] exposure time\n\njulia> card.value, card.units\n(1200.0, s)\n\njulia> units(card)\ns\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"RATE\", 3.5f0u\"km/s\", \"velocity\"))\nRATE    =                  3.5 / [km s-1] velocity\n\njulia> card = Card(\"RATE\", 3.5f0, \"[km/s] velocity\"); card.units, card.comment\n(km s⁻¹, \"velocity\")\n\njulia> show_(card)\nRATE    =                  3.5 / [km/s] velocity\n\njulia> card = Card(\"RATE\", 3.5f0, \"[foo] velocity\"); card.units, card.comment\n(nothing, \"[foo] velocity\")\n\nThe style of the unit string of the comment is written again, e.g., [km/s], and a Quantity is written in the default style. The comment of a string or a logical, of a mandatory keyword, and of a number with a Quantity, is not units, and text that is not a unit string or a scale factor remains in the comment.\n\nThe units are written as the strings of the FITS Standard (Sect. 4.3), by formunit, and are read by parseunit. All the units of Tables 3 and 4 of the Standard are read, with the prefixes of Table 5 for the units that take them, e.g., a, the Julian year, Ma, R, the rayleigh, mag, D, the debye, and Sun. A unit with a scale factor, e.g., 10**3 m, or without a name in the Standard, e.g., a function such as log(Hz), remains in the comment. The units of a card are not read if the keyword units = false is given to parse.","title":"Units"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Fixed-and-free-format","page":"Cards","text":"The values of a card are in fixed format by default, where a number ends at byte 30 and a string begins at byte 11. A number has at most 20 characters in fixed format, so a Float64 that has more characters is written with fewer digits of the mantissa. The free format has no such limit.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\"; fixed = false))\nEXPTIME = 1200.0               / exposure time\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"DELTA\", 1.2345678901234567e-100))\nDELTA   = 1.2345678901234D-100\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"DELTA\", 1.2345678901234567e-100; fixed = false))\nDELTA   = 1.2345678901234567D-100\n\nThe mandatory keywords SIMPLE, XTENSION, BITPIX, NAXIS, NAXISn, PCOUNT, GCOUNT, and END are always in fixed format and have no units.\n\nThe position of the comment is the keyword slash, the byte of the comment separator, which is 32 by default, and lpad, rpad, and upad are the numbers of spaces before and after the separator and after the units. A comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card is truncated, and the comment of the card is the truncated comment, unless truncate = false, which is an ArgumentError.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\"; slash = 40))\nEXPTIME =               1200.0         / exposure time\n\njulia> Card(\"A\", 1, repeat(\"x\", 100)).comment |> length\n47\n\njulia> Card(\"A\", 1, repeat(\"x\", 100); truncate = false)\nERROR: ArgumentError: Comment does not fit on the card.\n[...]","title":"Fixed and free format"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Commentary-cards","page":"Cards","text":"A COMMENT, a HISTORY, and a blank keyword have no value indicator and only text.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"COMMENT\", \"A comment\"))\nCOMMENT A comment\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"HISTORY\", \"processed\"))\nHISTORY processed\n\njulia> card = Card(\"COMMENT\", \"A comment\"); card.value\n\"A comment\"","title":"Commentary cards"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#HIERARCH-keywords","page":"Cards","text":"A keyword that is longer than 8 characters is a HIERARCH keyword, where the tokens of the keyword follow HIERARCH. A warning is given, unless warn = false. The key of the card is the tokens.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"HIERARCH\", \"ESO TEL FOCU SCALE\", 1.489, \"(deg/m) Focus\");\n\njulia> show_(card)\nHIERARCH ESO TEL FOCU SCALE = 1.489 / (deg/m) Focus\n\njulia> card.key, typeof(card)\n(\"ESO TEL FOCU SCALE\", Card{Hierarch{Float64}})\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"LONGKEYWORD\", 1.5; warn = false))\nHIERARCH LONGKEYWORD = 1.5\n\nThe tokens have the characters of a keyword, and a HIERARCH card is in free format. A card without a value, e.g., HIERARCH some text, is commentary text.","title":"HIERARCH keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Long-strings","page":"Cards","text":"A string of at most 68 characters, with the doubled single quotes, is on one card, and is not split. A string that passes the comment separator moves the separator and the comment to the right, and the comment is truncated if it does not fit on the card, unless truncate = false, where the comment is continued on CONTINUE cards.\n\njulia> show_(Card(\"DATE-OBS\", \"2020-01-01T00:00:00.000\", \"observation date\"))\nDATE-OBS= '2020-01-01T00:00:00.000' / observation date\n\njulia> card = Card(\"KEY\", repeat(\"x\", 60), \"a comment that is truncated\"); card.comment\n\"a com\"\n\njulia> cards = Card(\"KEY\", repeat(\"x\", 60), \"a comment that is continued\"; truncate = false);\n\njulia> foreach(show_, cards)\nKEY     = 'xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx&' / a\nCONTINUE  ''                   / comment that is continued\n\nThe spaces at the ends of a comment are not significant, so they are not part of the comment of a card. The comments of the cards of a long string are joined by join_cards with a space, unless a comment ends with a space.\n\nA string that does not fit on one card is split into the cards of a long string, a card and the CONTINUE cards (Sect. 4.2.1.2), which are returned as a vector. Every card but the last ends with an ampersand. The string is split at the comment separator, or at the end of the card if there is no comment. A string is split after the last space that fits, unless the keyword splitat = nothing is given, where it is split at any character, or the keyword splitat is another character.\n\njulia> cards = Card(\"LONGSTR\", repeat(\"This is a long string value. \", 5), \"comment\");\n\njulia> foreach(show_, cards)\nLONGSTR = 'This is a long &'   / comment\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. &'\nCONTINUE  'This is a long &'\nCONTINUE  'string value. '\n\nThe comment is split in the same way. A list of cards is joined into the value of the card by join_cards.\n\njulia> join_cards(cards).value\n\"This is a long string value. This is a long string value. This is a long string value. This is a long string value. This is a long string value. \"","title":"Long strings"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Parsing-an-image","page":"Cards","text":"parse(Card, image) creates a card from an 80-character image. The type of the card follows the image: a value, a HIERARCH keyword, commentary text, a CONTINUE card, an END card, or an invalid record.\n\njulia> image = rpad(\"EXPTIME = 1200. / [s] exposure time in seconds\", 80);\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, image);\n\njulia> card.key, card.value, card.units, card.comment\n(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0f0, s, \"exposure time in seconds\")\n\njulia> repr(card) == image\ntrue\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"COMMENT some text\", 80)))\nCard{Comment}\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"END\", 80)))\nCard{End}\n\nThe type of a number is the smallest type that holds the number, and a floating-point number is a Float32 if it is exactly the shortest Float32 of its text. An integer is an Int64, an Int128, or a BigInt.\n\nA record with a malformed value is an Invalid card, which keeps the text of the record and is written as it was read. Only a record that is not a value, e.g., a keyword without the value indicator = in bytes 9 and 10, is commentary text.\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1 2 3\", 80));\n\njulia> typeof(card), card.key\n(Card{Invalid}, \"KEY\")\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"HIERARCH ESO A = 1 2 3\", 80)))\nCard{Invalid}\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY=  1\", 80)))\nCard{Invalid}\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"HIERARCH some text\", 80)))\nCard{Comment}\n\nThe Standard allows an upper case E or D for the exponent. A number with a lower case letter is an invalid card, unless the keyword strict = false is given.\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.5e3\", 80)))\nCard{Invalid}\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.5e3\", 80); strict = false);\n\njulia> card.value\n1500.0f0\n\njulia> show_(card)\nKEY     = 1.5E3\n\nA CONTINUE card continues a string only if the card before it is a string that ends with an ampersand, so the card before it is given to parse. A CONTINUE card that does not continue a string, and a CONTINUE record without spaces in bytes 9 and 10 or without a string, is commentary.\n\njulia> images = [rpad(\"LONGSTR = 'This is a long &' / comment\", 80), rpad(\"CONTINUE  'string value.'\", 80)];\n\njulia> cards = Card{<:Any}[]; previous = nothing;\n\njulia> for image in images\n           card = parse(Card, image, previous)\n           push!(cards, card)\n           previous = card\n       end\n\njulia> typeof.(cards)\n2-element Vector{DataType}:\n Card{Value{String}}\n Card{Continue}\n\njulia> join_cards(cards).value\n\"This is a long string value.\"\n\njulia> typeof(parse(Card, rpad(\"CONTINUE  'orphan'\", 80), nothing))\nCard{Comment}","title":"Parsing an image"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Reading-and-writing-a-card","page":"Cards","text":"A card is written as it was read, and the image of a parsed card is the image that was read, including the format of the numbers, the position of the values, the units, and the comment. The exceptions are those of the reading, where the value is not changed:\n\na number without an integer digit has a zero, e.g., .5 is 0.5;\nan exponent has at least two digits, and a mantissa has one integer digit, e.g., E+007 is E+07 and 56.53E+13 is 5.653E+14;\nthe sign of an integer zero is lost;\na number that is longer than before takes the spaces before the comment, but may move the comment, and the end of the comment of a full card is lost.\n\nTrailing spaces of a string are not significant, and are restored in the written card.","title":"Reading and writing a card"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Changing-the-value","page":"Cards","text":"setvalue! returns the card with a new value, and the units, format, and comment of the card. The new value may have another type. A number keeps the format of the old number, e.g., the digits and the exponent letter, only if the number is written with that format without a loss of digits. A number without units has the units of the card.\n\njulia> card = Card(\"EXPTIME\", 1200.0, \"exposure time\");\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 1500.0))\nEXPTIME =               1500.0 / exposure time\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 1500.0u\"s\"))\nEXPTIME =               1500.0 / [s] exposure time\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(Card(\"N\", 1, \"c\"), \"text\"))\nN       = 'text'               / c\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.50 / c\", 80));\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5))\nKEY     = 2.50 / c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.567))\nKEY     = 2.567 / c\n\nA string that does not fit is split into the cards of a long string, as by Card. The value of a commentary card is a string or missing, and an END card has no value, otherwise an ArgumentError is raised.\n\nThe keywords of setvalue! are the keywords of Card that format the card, and the default of a layout keyword is the layout of the card, so the new card is formatted as the card was.\n\nKeyword Default\nfixed the format of the card, fixed or free\nslash the index of the comment separator of the card, and for the cards of a long string the index 32 or more\nlpad 0 if the separator follows the value of the card directly, otherwise 1\nrpad the number of spaces after the separator of the card, or 1\nupad the number of spaces after the units of the card, or 1\nappend false\ntruncate true, as in Card\nsplitat ' ', as in Card\n\nA keyword formats the card again, even if the value is the same, and a keyword creates the same card as the constructor of a card with the keyword.\n\njulia> card = parse(Card, rpad(\"KEY     = 1.5/ c\", 80));\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5))\nKEY     = 2.5/ c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5; lpad = 3))\nKEY     = 2.5   / c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5; slash = 20))\nKEY     = 2.5      / c\n\njulia> show_(setvalue!(card, 2.5; fixed = true))\nKEY     =                  2.5/ c\n\nA list of cards in a header is changed by setvalue!(cards, key, value) and by cards[key] = value, which also replace the CONTINUE cards of the old value.","title":"Changing the value"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#A-list-of-cards","page":"Cards","text":"A header is a vector of cards, and the cards with a keyword are found by the keyword, without regard to the case.\n\njulia> cards = Card{<:Any}[Card(\"BITPIX\", 16), Card(\"BZERO\", 1.5), Card(\"OBJECT\", \"NGC 1275\"),\n                           Card(\"HISTORY\", \"first step\"), Card(\"HISTORY\", \"second step\")];\n\njulia> cards[\"bitpix\"], get(cards, \"NOPE\", 0), haskey(cards, \"OBJECT\")\n(16, 0, true)\n\njulia> get(cards, [\"BITPIX\", \"BZERO\", \"NOPE\"], [0, 0, \"default\"])\n(16, 1.5, \"default\")\n\njulia> FITSFiles.getall(cards, \"HISTORY\")\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"first step\"\n \"second step\"\n\njulia> findall(\"HISTORY\", cards)\n2-element Vector{Int64}:\n 4\n 5\n\nThe value of a card is the first card with the keyword, for getindex, get, and haskey, and findfirst is the index of the first card. With a list of keys, get is the tuple of the values of the first cards, where the types of the values are the types of the cards and not of the defaults. findall and getall are the indexes and the values of all the cards with a keyword, e.g., the COMMENT and HISTORY cards. The value of a continued string is the joined string, and setvalue!(cards, key, value) replaces the card and the CONTINUE cards of the old string.","title":"A list of cards"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Comparing-and-copying","page":"Cards","text":"Two cards are equal if their images are equal, so a card is equal to the card that is parsed from its image, and equal cards have the same hash. A copy has its own format, so the copy can be changed without changing the card.\n\njulia> a, b = Card(\"A\", 1, \"c\"), Card(\"A\", 1, \"c\");\n\njulia> a == b, hash(a) == hash(b), copy(a) == a, copy(a) === a\n(true, true, true, false)\n\njulia> parse(Card, repr(a)) == a\ntrue\n\nA card is a scalar in a broadcast, e.g., cards .== a.","title":"Comparing and copying"},{"category":"section","location":"cards/#Errors","page":"Cards","text":"Error Cause\nArgumentError a keyword, a string, or a comment with characters that are not allowed\nArgumentError a value of a type that cannot be written\nArgumentError a string value for a number, or a value that is not a string for a commentary or CONTINUE card\nArgumentError a comment, units, or a HIERARCH keyword and value that do not fit on the card\nDomainError a number that is not finite\n\nReading an image never raises an error. A record that is not valid is an Invalid card.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"#FITSFiles.jl","page":"FITSFiles","text":"FITSFiles.jl is a Julia implementation of the Flexible Image Transport System (FITS) file IO standard.\n\nA FITS file is a vector of header-data units (HDUs), where the header of an HDU is a vector of cards, and fits reads the file.","title":"FITSFiles.jl"},{"category":"module","location":"#FITSFiles.FITSFiles","page":"FITSFiles","text":"FITSFiles.jl is a Julia implementation of the Flexible Image Transport System (FITS) file format.\n\nFITS files are composed of a vector of header-data units (HDUs), where an HDU is a header, which is a vector of cards, followed by data.\n\nfits is the most common function for reading a file, and write writes it.\nHDU creates a header-data unit.\nCard creates, and parse reads, a card of a header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.FITSFiles"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.write-Tuple{IO, Vector{HDU}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.write(io::IO, hdus::Vector{HDU})\nBase.write(filename::AbstractString, hdus::Vector{HDU})\n\nWrite a vector of header-data units (HDUs) to a file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.write"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.fits-Tuple{IO}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"fits(io::IO; <keywords>)\nfits(filename::AbstractString; <keywords>)\n\nOpen and read a FITS file, returning a vector of header-data units (HDUs).\n\nThe default data structure for Random and Bintable HDUs is a named tuple of arrays.\n\nKeywords\n\nrecord::Bool=false: structure the data as a list of records\nscale::Bool=true: apply the scale and zero keywords to the data\nstrict::Bool=true: numbers with a lower case exponent letter (d or e) are invalid cards, unless false\nunits::Bool=true: the units in the comment of a number are the units of the value, unless false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.fits"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.info-Tuple{Vector{HDU}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"info(hdus::Vector{HDU})\n\nBriefly describe the list of header-data units (HDUs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.info"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Bintable","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Bintable header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Bintable HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a 2-dimensional array of binary data structured as a vector of records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Bintable"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.HDU","page":"FITSFiles","text":"HDU{T}(cards, data)\nHDU([data, [cards]]; <keywords>)\nHDU(cards; <keywords>)\nHDU(type, missing, [cards]; <keywords>)\nHDU(type, data, [cards]; <keywords>)\n\nCreate a header-data unit described by data and cards of type AbstractHDU.\n\nThe HDU function tries to determine the HDU type based on the data and cards. If only data is provided, then an array is interpreted as a Primary HDU and a Tuple or NamedTuple as a Bintable. If mandatory cards are provided, then they are used to determine the HDU type. Otherwise, the HDU type must be specified.\n\nHDU types are: Primary, Random, Image, Table, Bintable, and Conform.\n\nArguments\n\ndata::U=missing: the binary or ASCII data, where U <: Union{AbstractArray,  Tuple, NamedTuple, Missing}\ncards::U=missing: the list of cards, where U <: Union{Card, Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}, Missing}\n\nKeywords\n\nrecord::Bool=false: structure the data as a list of records\nscale::Bool=true: apply the scale and zero keywords to the data\n\nThe following keywords format the cards that are created, and are those of Card, which describes them.\n\nappend::Bool=false: append an ampersand to a string that is continued by a CONTINUE card\nfixed::Bool=true: create fixed format cards\nslash::Integer=32: index of the comment separator (/)\nlpad::Integer=1: number of spaces before the comment separator\nrpad::Integer=1: number of spaces after the comment separator\nupad::Integer=1: number of spaces after the units\ntruncate::Bool=true: truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.HDU"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Image","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Image header-data unit (HDU)\n\nAn Image HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a multidimensional array (<=999 dimensions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Image"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Primary","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Primary header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Primary HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a multidimensional array (<=999 dimensions). The Primary HDU is the first HDU in a vector of HDUs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Primary"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Random","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Random header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Random HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a vector of records containing a list of parameters followed by a multidimensional array. The Random HDU is the first HDU in a vector of HDUs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Random"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Table","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Table header-data unit (HDU)\n\nA Table HDU consists of a vector of cards and optionally a 2-dimensional array of ASCII data structured as vector of records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Table"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.findall-Tuple{AbstractString, Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"findall(key, cards)\n\nReturn the indexes of all the cards with the keyword key, in the order of the header. The indexes are empty if there is no such card.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.findall"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.get-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, Union{Tuple, AbstractArray}, Union{Tuple, AbstractArray}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"get(cards, key, default)\nget(cards, keys, defaults)\n\nReturn the value of the first card with the keyword key, or default if there is no such card. With a list of keys, return the tuple of the values of the first cards with the keywords, in the order of the keys, where the default of a keyword that is not found is the default at the same position. The types of the values are the types of the cards, and not the types of the defaults. The value of a continued string is the joined string. See getall for the values of all the cards with a keyword.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.get"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.read-Tuple{IO, Type{HDU}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.read(io::IO, ::Type{HDU}; <keywords>)\n\nRead the next header-data unit (HDU) from io and return it. The data of an HDU that is read from a file is a lazy array, which is read when the data is accessed, and the data of an HDU that is read from another stream is read at once.\n\nKeywords\n\ntype=nothing: the HDU type, e.g., Image, which is otherwise determined by the mandatory keywords of the header\nstrict::Bool=true: numbers with a lower case exponent letter (d or e) are invalid cards, unless false\nunits::Bool=true: the units in the comment of a number are the units of the value, unless false\nrecord::Bool=false: structure the data as a list of records\nscale::Bool=true: apply the scale and zero keywords to the data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.read"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.write-Tuple{IO, HDU}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.write(io::IO, hdu::HDU; <keywords>)\n\nWrite a header-data unit (HDU) to io: the header cards, the END card, and blank cards that pad the header to a multiple of 2880 bytes, followed by the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.write"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.getall-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"getall(cards, key)\ngetall(cards, keys)\n\nReturn the values of all the cards with the keyword key, in the order of the header, e.g., the values of the COMMENT or HISTORY cards, which are the vector of the values, or an empty vector if there is no such card. With a list of keys, return the tuple of the vectors of the keys. The value of a continued string is the joined string, and the CONTINUE cards of the string are not values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.getall"},{"category":"function","location":"#FITSFiles.info","page":"FITSFiles","text":"info(hdu::HDU)\n\nBriefly describe the header-data unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.info"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.joinvalue-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, Integer}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"joinvalue(cards, j)\n\nReturn the value of the card at index j. The value of a continued string is the value of the card joined with the CONTINUE cards that follow it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.joinvalue"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.leastfloat-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Real}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"leastfloat(real::Union{Real, Nothing})\n\nConvert an integer to the smallest float type of Float32, Float64, and BigFloat that holds the integer, i.e., a Float32 for a 32-bit integer, a Float64 for a 64-bit integer, and otherwise a BigFloat. A value that is not an integer is returned as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.leastfloat"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.setvalue!-Tuple{Union{Vector{Card}, Vector{Card{<:Any}}}, AbstractString, Union{Missing, AbstractString, Real, Complex, Unitful.Quantity}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"setvalue!(cards, key, value; <keywords>)\n\nReplace the value of the first card with the keyword key. A string that does not fit on a card is split into the cards of a long string, and the CONTINUE cards of a string that is replaced are removed. The keywords, i.e., fixed, append, slash, lpad, rpad, upad, truncate, and splitat, are those of setvalue!(card, value), where the default of a layout keyword is the layout of the card. The cards are always replaced, which is the way to split the value again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.setvalue!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.typeofhdu-Tuple{U} where U<:Union{Missing, Tuple, AbstractArray, NamedTuple}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"typeofhdu(data)\ntypeofhdu(dict)\ntypeofhdu(data, dict)\n\nDetermine the HDU type based on the data structure, the mandatory keywords, or both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.typeofhdu"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.units-Tuple{Card}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"units(card)\nunits(cards, key)\n\nReturn the units of a card, or of the first card of a list of cards with the keyword, which are nothing if the value has no units. The units are not part of the value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.units"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.DataFormat","page":"FITSFiles","text":"DataFormat(type, leng, shape, param, group, heap)\n\nThe generic data format descriptor for HDUs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.DataFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.LazyArray","page":"FITSFiles","text":"LazyArray(filnam, mtime, begpos, format, fields, keywds)\n\nThe lazy array file descriptor of the data of an HDU, which is read from the file when the data is accessed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.LazyArray"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ImageField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ImageField(type, zero, scale, miss, dmin, dmax)\n\nImage array element descriptor (applicable to Primary and Image HDUs)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ImageField"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.ConformField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"ConformField()\n\nConforming array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.ConformField"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.RandomField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"RandomField(name, index, type, slice, leng, shape, zero, scale)\n\nRandom array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.RandomField"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.TableField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"TableField(name, type, slice, unit, form, disp, zero, scale, null,\n    dmin, dmax, lmin, lmax)\n\nTable array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.TableField"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.BinaryField","page":"FITSFiles","text":"BinaryField(name, pntr, type, slice, leng, supp, unit, disp, dims,\n    zero, scale, null, dmin, dmax, lmin, lmax)\n\nBinary array element descriptor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.BinaryField"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.Card","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Card(type, key, value, [units,] comment, format)\nCard([key, [value, [comment]]]; <keywords>)\nCard(\"HIERARCH\", tokens, value, comment; <keywords>)\n\nCreate a card type, where type is Comment, Continue, End, Hierarch, History, Invalid, or Value{T}.\n\nArguments\n\nkey::AbstractString=\"\": keyword string\nvalue::U=missing: keyword value, where U is Missing, Bool, Integer, AbstractFloat, a Complex number of integers or floats, or AbstractString, which is the type T of the card Value{T}. Another type of value is an ArgumentError. A Quantity of a number may be given in place of the number, but a Quantity is not a type of value: the value of the card is the number, and the units of the Quantity are the units of the card. The units of a number without a Quantity are the unit string in square brackets at the beginning of the comment, e.g., [km/s] velocity, as they are for an image, and the unit string is removed from the comment. The comment of a string, a logical, a mandatory keyword, text that is not a unit string, or a scale factor, is not units.\ntokens::AbstractString: the tokens of a HIERARCH keyword, which are separated by spaces\ncomment::AbstractString=\"\": comment string\n\nKeywords\n\nappend::Bool=false: append an ampersand to the string value, which marks that the string is continued by a CONTINUE card. A string that is longer than the card (>68 characters) is always split into a card and CONTINUE cards, which are returned as a vector.\nfixed::Bool=true: use fixed format. A fixed-format number ends at byte 30, so a Float64 that has more than 20 characters is written with fewer digits of the mantissa, which are truncated, e.g., 1.2345678901234567e-100 is written as 1.2345678901234D-100. Use fixed=false to keep all of the digits.\nslash::Integer=32: index of the comment separator (/). A value that passes the separator moves the separator to the right. A number in fixed format ends at the index 30, so the separator is at the index 32 or later. A comment, and the units, must end at or before the end of the card. If there is no space for the comment after the separator, the comment is dropped, or is continued at the index 32 on the CONTINUE cards of a string if truncate is false.\nlpad::Integer=1: number of spaces before comment separator\nrpad::Integer=1: number of spaces after comment separator\nupad::Integer=1: number of spaces after the units string\ntruncate::Bool=true: truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card, and the comment of the card is the truncated comment. If false, the comment of a string is continued on CONTINUE cards of the string, and a comment of another value that does not fit, and units that do not fit, are an ArgumentError. A comment is dropped if the comment separator is at the end of the card.\nwarn::Bool=true: warn when a keyword longer than 8 characters creates a HIERARCH card\nsplitat::Union{Char, Nothing}=' ': split a long string and its comment into the cards after the last character that fits, instead of splitting at any character if nothing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.Card"},{"category":"type","location":"#FITSFiles.CardFormat","page":"FITSFiles","text":"CardFormat(fixd, vbeg, vend, ampr, slsh, ubeg, uend, cbeg, cend)\n\nCreate a card format descriptor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.CardFormat"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parse_complex-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parse_complex(real::AbstractString, imag::AbstractString)\n\nDetermine the type of a complex number as the smallest type that holds both parts. The parts are integers if both are integers, and otherwise floats, where an integer is a float of a type that holds the integer exactly, i.e., a Float32, a Float64, or a BigFloat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parse_complex"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parse_number-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parse_number(real::AbstractString)\n\nDetermine the type of a number as a Float32, Float64, Int64, Int128, or BigInt. A floating-point number is a Float32 only if it is represented exactly by its shortest Float32 string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parse_number"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.printfmt-Tuple{AbstractString, Number}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"printfmt(vfmt, value)\n\nReturn the text of a number with the printf format vfmt. The conversion e is for a number with a D exponent, which is written as a D. The format may end with |-, which writes the exponent with a sign only if it is negative and without leading zeros, e.g., E6.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.printfmt"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.join_cards-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"join_cards(cards)\n\nJoin CONTINUE cards to initial long string card to create a long value and comment card. Cards after the end of the continued string are ignored. The comments of the cards are joined with a space, unless a comment ends with a space, since the spaces at the end of the comment of an image are not read, e.g., a comment that is split at any character has a space at the split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.join_cards"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.setvalue!-Tuple{Card, Union{Missing, AbstractString, Real, Complex, Unitful.Quantity}}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"setvalue!(card, value; <keywords>)\n\nReturn the card with the value value, and the units, format, and comment of the card. A number without units has the units of the card, or the units at the beginning of the comment of the card if the card has no units, as for the constructor of a card. A string that fits on the card, i.e., that has at most 68 characters, is not split, and moves the comment to the right if the string passes the comment separator. A string that does not fit on the card is split into the cards of a long string, the first card and the CONTINUE cards, which are returned as a vector, at the comment separator, or at the end of the card if there is no comment. The keywords are those of the constructor of a card, where the default of a layout keyword is the layout of the card.\n\nKeywords\n\nfixed::Bool: use fixed format. The default is the format of the card.\nappend::Bool=false: append an ampersand to the string value, which marks that the string is continued by a CONTINUE card.\nslash::Integer: index of the comment separator (/). The default is the index of the separator of the card, and the index 32 or more for the separator of the cards of a long string.\nlpad::Integer: number of spaces before the comment separator. The default is 0 if the separator follows the value of the card directly, and otherwise 1.\nrpad::Integer: number of spaces after the comment separator. The default is the number of spaces of the card, or 1 if the card has no comment.\nupad::Integer: number of spaces after the units string. The default is the number of spaces of the card, or 1 if the card has no units.\ntruncate::Bool=true: truncate a comment that is longer than the space at the end of the card, and otherwise continue the comment on the CONTINUE cards of a string, or raise an ArgumentError for another value.\nsplitat::Union{Char, Nothing}=' ': split a long string and its comment after the last character that fits, or at any character if nothing.\n\nThe card is replaced by the new card when a layout keyword is given, even if the value is the same.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.setvalue!"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.split_card-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{K, S, C, Any}} where {K<:AbstractString, S<:AbstractString, C<:AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"split_card(key, value, comment, format)\n\nSplit card having long value and comment fields into multiple cards\n\nIf length of comment field is 0 because comment separator index is too large, then comment is deleted.  Decreasing comment separator index will allow inclusion of comment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.split_card"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Card}, AbstractString, Any}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.parse(::Type{Card}, image::AbstractString, previous; strict = true, units = true)\n\nParse 80 character card image that follows the card previous, or nothing. A CONTINUE card that does not continue a string is commentary text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Card}, AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"Base.parse(::Type{Card}, image::AbstractString; strict = true, units = true)\n\nParse 80 character card image from string buffer. A number with a lower case exponent letter (d or e) is not allowed by the standard, so it is an invalid card, unless strict is false. The units of a number, i.e., a unit string in square brackets at the beginning of the comment, are the units of the value, unless units is false or the keyword is mandatory.\n\nThe card is written as it is read, with the following exceptions, where the value is not changed: a number without an integer digit has a zero, e.g., .5 is 0.5; an exponent has at least two digits and a mantissa has one integer digit, e.g., E+007 is E+07 and 56.53E+13 is 5.653E+14; the sign of an integer zero is lost; and a number that is longer than before takes the spaces before the comment, but may move the comment, and the end of the comment of a full card is lost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"function","location":"#FITSFiles.formunit","page":"FITSFiles","text":"formunit(unit[, ufmt])\n\nReturn the ASCII string of a unit in the FITS standard (Sect. 4.3). The style of the string is ufmt, which has three characters: the separator of the units, a space, ., or *; / if a unit with a negative power follows a slash, or - if it has the negative power; and the style of a power, d for digits following the unit, ^, or * for **. The default is -d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.formunit"},{"category":"method","location":"#FITSFiles.parseunit-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"FITSFiles","text":"parseunit(string)\n\nParse a unit string of the FITS standard (Sect. 4.3) and return the unit, or nothing if the string is not a valid unit string or has a unit that is not known. Units are multiplied by a space, ., or *, and divided by /, which may begin the string. A unit is raised to a power by an integer following the unit, by a decimal number or a ratio in parentheses following the unit, or by ** or ^ followed by an integer, a decimal number, or a ratio in parentheses. A scale factor, 10**k, 10^k, or 10+k, is followed by a unit and returns a quantity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FITSFiles.parseunit"}]
}
